  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two toroidal magnetic-confinement device layouts for plasma. In both cases, a purple plasma column is contained inside a gray torus-shaped vessel and surrounded by copper-colored magnetic coils. The key message is how different coil arrangements create the magnetic fields needed to confine the plasma.\n\nOn the left, the device appears tokamak-like: large toroidal field coils wrap around the ring, while separate inner and outer poloidal field coils are added around the center and outside of the vessel. This combination suggests that toroidal and poloidal fields are produced by distinct coil systems to shape and stabilize the plasma inside the torus.\n\nOn the right, the device appears stellarator-like: the toroidal field coils are still present, but the additional shaping comes from helical field coils that wind around the torus in a twisted path. The purple plasma itself also takes on a visibly twisted, non-axisymmetric shape, matching the helical magnetic structure.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights a structural contrast between two approaches to magnetic confinement: one using separate poloidal coils around a more symmetric plasma, and one using helical coils to produce a twisted confinement geometry.",
